Q: How left join works with sqlx A: <p>Here's what I got.</p>
<h2>Using Absolute Paths</h2>
<p>I have a struct like so:</p>
<pre><code>type Posts struct {
    Id            string         `json:&quot;id&quot; db:&quot;posts.id&quot;`
    Uuid          string         `json:&quot;uuid&quot; db:&quot;posts.uuid&quot;`
    Title         string         `json:&quot;title&quot; db:&quot;posts.title&quot;`
    Author        *Users         `json:&quot;author&quot; db:&quot;&quot;`
    // ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>Which references <code>Users</code>:</p>
<pre><code>type Users struct {
    Id           string         `json:&quot;id&quot; db:&quot;users.id&quot;`
    Name         string         `json:&quot;name&quot; db:&quot;users.name&quot;`
    Slug         string         `json:&quot;slug&quot; db:&quot;users.slug&quot;`
    Email        string         `json:&quot;email&quot; db:&quot;users.email&quot;`
    // ...
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Very important to note if you are using MySQL: the column names being returned by MySQL do <strong>not</strong> include the table names (i.e., <code>users.id</code>) unless you enable <a href="https://github.com/go-sql-driver/MYSQL#columnswithalias" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>columnsWithAlias</code></a> in your connection string.</h3>
<h5>To make this work, we are giving sqlx an empty tag for <code>Authors</code> so that it uses the full tag names in <code>Users</code> directly.</h5>
<p>In my generic query builder function, the columns get specified with the table names for the select and joins (the <code>colName</code> is what's important):</p>
<pre><code>func (q *Query[T]) BuildSelectQuery() (err error) {
    var t T

    var table string

    typeOf := reflect.TypeOf(t).String()
    typeOfSplit := strings.Split(typeOf, &quot;.&quot;)
    if len(typeOfSplit) &gt; 1 {
        table = strings.ToLower(typeOfSplit[1])
    }

    var cols []string
    for _, c := range q.Columns {
        colName := fmt.Sprintf(&quot;%s.%s&quot;, table, c) // table_name.column
        cols = append(cols, colName)
    }
    // ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you have no duplicate field names between multiple tables, or if you want to just alias specific columns, you can leave <code>columnsWithAlias</code> off and just treat all of the resulting columns as flat column names that will match up to any nested structs with their tags.</p>
<h2>Using Traversal</h2>
<p>This is more like what you see in the sqlx documentation, where there are examples with embedded structs, meaning the fields are top-level in the struct and cannot overlap.  This is similar, but we create a query response struct that we unpack after the scan, keeping all fields separate.</p>
<pre><code>type PostsQueryResponse struct {
    Post   *Posts `db:&quot;posts&quot;`
    Author *Users `db:&quot;users&quot;`
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now the column names are normal for the actual data structs:</p>
<pre><code>type Posts struct {
    Id            string         `json:&quot;id&quot; db:&quot;id&quot;`
    Uuid          string         `json:&quot;uuid&quot; db:&quot;uuid&quot;`
    Title         string         `json:&quot;title&quot; db:&quot;title&quot;`
    // ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>This gives it a root struct and paths to follow for <code>posts</code> and <code>users</code>.  If it doesn't have the root struct for the response, it won't know to follow <code>posts</code> for the base table name.</p>
<p>You still need the table names for the query, and now you have to unpack Author:</p>
<pre><code>for _, i := range r {
    i.Post.Author = i.Author
    posts = append(posts, *i.Post)
}
</code></pre>
<p>I kind of like the absolute paths better, but it's good to have options and to understand different ways sqlx is meant to work.</p>

Q: How can I encode the filename parameter of Content-Disposition header in HTTP? A: <p>If you are using a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Node.js" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Node.js</a> backend, you can use the following code I found <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/encodeURIComponent%23Examples" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var fileName = 'my file(2).txt';
var header = &quot;Content-Disposition: attachment; filename*=UTF-8''&quot;
             + encodeRFC5987ValueChars(fileName);

function encodeRFC5987ValueChars (str) {
    return encodeURIComponent(str).
        // Note that although RFC 3986 reserves &quot;!&quot;, RFC 5987 does not,
        // so we do not need to escape it
        replace(/['()]/g, escape). // i.e., %27 %28 %29
        replace(/\*/g, '%2A').
            // The following are not required for percent-encoding per RFC 5987,
            // so we can allow for a little better readability over the wire: |`^
            replace(/%(?:7C|60|5E)/g, unescape);
}
</code></pre>

Q: scroll driven image sequence animation only plays first animation A: <p>One of the ways is to</p>
<ol>
<li>have multiple hero-sequence classes (so as to allow the system to calculate the offset on scroll for the three animations</li>
<li>use multiple IDs for the canvas (e.g. canvas1, canvas2, canvas3)</li>
<li>use multiple variables for the three different instances</li>
</ol>
<p>(1) Keeping the original code structure but (2) applying the above three changes, the code (for three animations) is</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;meta charset=&quot;utf-8&quot;&gt;
&lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1&quot;&gt;

&lt;style&gt;
body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: -apple-system, sans-serif;
    color: #1d1d1f;
}

h1 {
    font-size: 40px;
    margin: 0 0 10px;
}

p {
    font-size: 22px;
}

.hero {
    padding: 40px;
    text-align: center;
}

.hero-sequence, .hero-sequence2 , .hero-sequence3 {
    height: 430vh;
}

.sticky-element {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    height: 100vh;
    min-height: 1033px;
    overflow: hidden;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
}

.sequence-element {
    width: 1336px;
    height: 786px;
}

footer {
    background: #f5f5f7;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 40px;
    height: 400px;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
}
&lt;/style&gt;

&lt;title&gt;Apple Sequence Animation&lt;/title&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;hero&quot;&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Apple Sequence Animation&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Scroll down to admire the magic.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;div class=&quot;hero-sequence&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;sticky-element&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div class=&quot;sequence-element&quot;&gt;
            &lt;canvas width=&quot;1336&quot; height=&quot;786&quot; id=canvas1&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;hero-sequence2&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;sticky-element&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div class=&quot;sequence-element&quot;&gt;
            &lt;canvas width=&quot;1336&quot; height=&quot;786&quot; id=canvas2&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;hero-sequence3&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;sticky-element&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div class=&quot;sequence-element&quot;&gt;
            &lt;canvas width=&quot;1336&quot; height=&quot;786&quot; id=canvas3&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;footer&gt;
    You are awesome!
&lt;/footer&gt;

&lt;script&gt;
const canvas = document.getElementById('canvas1')
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d')

const canvas2 = document.getElementById('canvas2')
const ctx2 = canvas2.getContext('2d')

const canvas3 = document.getElementById('canvas3')
const ctx3 = canvas3.getContext('2d')

const heroSequence = document.querySelector('.hero-sequence')
const heroSequence2 = document.querySelector('.hero-sequence2')
const heroSequence3 = document.querySelector('.hero-sequence3')

const images = []
const frameCount = 87

const prepareImages = () =&gt; {
    for (var i = 0; i &lt; frameCount; i++) {
      const image = new Image()
      image.src = `https://spharian.vercel.app/lab/apple-sequence-animation/images/${i}.jpg`
      images.push(image)

      if (i === 0) {
        images[i].onload = () =&gt; drawImage(0)
      }
    }
}


const drawImage = frameIndex =&gt; {
    ctx.drawImage(images[frameIndex], 0, 0)
}

const drawImage2 = frameIndex =&gt; {
    ctx2.drawImage(images[frameIndex], 0, 0)
}

const drawImage3 = frameIndex =&gt; {
    ctx3.drawImage(images[frameIndex], 0, 0)
}


prepareImages();

window.addEventListener('scroll', () =&gt; {


// instance 1
    const scrollTop = document.documentElement.scrollTop - heroSequence.offsetTop
    const maxScrollTop = heroSequence.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight
    const scrollFraction = scrollTop / maxScrollTop
    const frameIndex = Math.max(0, Math.min(frameCount - 1, Math.ceil(scrollFraction * frameCount)))
    images[frameIndex].onload = () =&gt; drawImage(frameIndex)
    requestAnimationFrame(() =&gt; drawImage(frameIndex))

// instance 2
    const scrollTop2 = document.documentElement.scrollTop - heroSequence2.offsetTop
    const maxScrollTop2 = heroSequence2.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight
    const scrollFraction2 = scrollTop2 / maxScrollTop2
    const frameIndex2 = Math.max(0, Math.min(frameCount - 1, Math.ceil(scrollFraction2 * frameCount)))
    images[frameIndex].onload = () =&gt; drawImage2(frameIndex)
    requestAnimationFrame(() =&gt; drawImage2(frameIndex2))


// instance 3
    const scrollTop3 = document.documentElement.scrollTop - heroSequence3.offsetTop
    const maxScrollTop3 = heroSequence3.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight
    const scrollFraction3 = scrollTop3 / maxScrollTop3
    const frameIndex3 = Math.max(0, Math.min(frameCount - 1, Math.ceil(scrollFraction3 * frameCount)))
    images[frameIndex].onload = () =&gt; drawImage3(frameIndex)
    requestAnimationFrame(() =&gt; drawImage3(frameIndex3))

})
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>See <a href="https://www.createchhk.com/SOanswers/so-html-26Apr2026.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">demo</a></p>
<p>NOTE:</p>
<p>I have used the same image array for all he three animations, in real case I believe you need to amend the code to use three different image arrays.</p>

